# Local dev DB (run `./scripts/db-up.sh` to start Postgres in Docker).
# Host port is 5433 (not 5432) to avoid conflicting with other local
# Postgres instances. The container internally uses 5432.
#
# This connects as `prodect`, the Docker-image superuser. A superuser has an
# implicit BYPASSRLS attribute, so the workspace RLS policies are INERT on this
# connection (they exist but never execute). That is acceptable for v1: the
# primary tenant-isolation defense is application-layer (always going through
# withWorkspaceContext + filtering by workspaceId); RLS is the secondary,
# defense-in-depth layer. See PRODECT_FINDINGS #5(deploy).
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://prodect:prodect@localhost:5433/prodect"

# ── The RLS-enforcing runtime role, and the TWO-URL contract ────────────────
#
# `motir_app` is the role the application is meant to connect as: created
# NOSUPERUSER NOBYPASSRLS by the workspace-RLS migration, granted LOGIN by
# 20260528175528_grant_prodect_app_login, and renamed from `prodect_app` by
# 20260810000000_rename_app_role_to_motir_app. Connecting as it is what makes the
# workspace RLS policies actually execute — a superuser has BYPASSRLS implicitly,
# which overrides even FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY.
#
# THE CONTRACT IS TWO URLS FOR TWO JOBS, and they are not interchangeable:
#
#   DATABASE_URL          -> motir_app. The RUNTIME connection. Restricted on
#                            purpose: the policies bite, and it holds no DDL
#                            rights.
#   DATABASE_URL_UNPOOLED -> the owner. What `prisma migrate deploy` runs as
#                            (prisma.config.ts prefers it), because migrations
#                            need the DDL rights the runtime role must not have.
#
# Locally, both point at the superuser above and the policies are INERT — which
# is fine for dev, and is why the suite has an opt-in switch rather than a
# silent difference:
#
#   TEST_DB_APP_ROLE=1 pnpm vitest run <files>
#
# runs the CODE UNDER TEST as motir_app while fixtures and teardown stay on the
# owner (tests/helpers/adminDb.ts). `globalSetup` provisions the role's
# test-only password itself, so this needs no setup and no secret. Unset — the
# default, and what CI runs — nothing changes. Note the whole suite does not yet
# pass under the flag; MOTIR-2514 is measuring how much of it does.
#
# To point a local app at the role by hand instead:
#   DATABASE_URL="postgresql://motir_app:motir_app@localhost:5433/prodect"
#
# IN PRODUCTION, never that literal password. Each deployed environment injects
# motir_app's credentials from its own secret store, and pointing the deployed
# DATABASE_URL at the role is MOTIR-2515 — a deliberate, verified cutover, not a
# config edit. (This paragraph used to defer that work to "Story 1.5's deploy
# hardening", a card that never existed; MOTIR-2435 is the tracking it lacked.)

# Auth — Better-Auth (Story 1.1)
# BETTER_AUTH_SECRET: required. Used to sign session cookies. Generate a
# fresh 32-byte secret for each environment with:
#   openssl rand -base64 32
# In production, store this in the Vercel dashboard (Settings → Environment
# Variables). Rotating the secret invalidates all active sessions.
BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=

# MOTIR_BASE_URL: the application's own absolute origin — the ONE variable
# that carries it (docs/decisions/application-hosting.md §4 / Q3). Everything
# that builds an absolute URL back to this app resolves through it: Better-Auth
# callbacks and email links, OAuth round-trips, public-project canonical URLs,
# and the automation email CTA. Optional in local dev — lib/baseUrl.ts falls
# back to http://localhost:3000 — but MUST be set in every deployed environment
# to the canonical public origin (production: https://app.motir.co).
#
# It replaced BETTER_AUTH_URL plus Vercel's injected VERCEL_URL /
# VERCEL_BRANCH_URL / VERCEL_PROJECT_PRODUCTION_URL. There is no second rung:
# a platform-injected per-deployment URL is precisely what the move off preview
# environments removes.
# MOTIR_BASE_URL=http://localhost:3000

# Email provider — see lib/email.ts for the abstraction.
# 'console' (default) prints emails to stdout so dev/test flows can grep
# the reset link. 'file' appends them to EMAIL_OUTBOX_PATH for the E2E suite
# and is refused in production. 'resend' is the WIRED production provider
# (MOTIR-1127) — it needs the two values below. 'postmark' is still a stub and
# throws at module load with a clear message if selected.
#
# Nothing here is specific to Motir's own vendor account: a self-hoster points
# the same three variables at their own Resend workspace, or leaves
# EMAIL_PROVIDER unset and keeps 'console'.
EMAIL_PROVIDER=console

# Required when (and only when) EMAIL_PROVIDER=resend — read once at module
# load, so a deployment missing either fails at BOOT with a message naming the
# variable, never on the first password reset.
#
#   RESEND_API_KEY — a SEND-ONLY Resend API key. Motir only ever POSTs
#     /emails; it never reads a message back, so the key needs no read scope
#     (a send-only key answers a read with 401 restricted_api_key).
#   EMAIL_FROM     — the sender identity, e.g. "Motir <no-reply@motir.co>".
#     Its domain must be verified in Resend (SPF + DKIM), or every send comes
#     back 422 invalid_from_address. The address needs no mailbox to send as.
#
# In production these are secrets on the deployment, never committed and never
# a build argument (on Fly: `flyctl secrets set -a motir-core RESEND_API_KEY=…`).
# RESEND_API_KEY=
# EMAIL_FROM=Motir <no-reply@motir.co>

# OPTIONAL — how many NOTIFICATION emails (watchers, mentions, subscriptions,
# digests) may be sent per rolling 24 hours (MOTIR-5873). Every template shares
# the provider account's quota; this cap keeps a notification burst from
# spending the room password-reset, invite and sign-in mail needs, which are
# never capped. Unset: 60 with EMAIL_PROVIDER=resend (under the Resend free
# plan's 100/day), no cap otherwise. An integer ≥ 0, or `none` on a plan with no
# daily quota. Anything else fails the boot.
# EMAIL_NOTIFICATION_DAILY_BUDGET=60

# OPTIONAL, and independent of the two above — the inbound DELIVERY webhook
# (MOTIR-3515). Read at CALL time rather than module load, so leaving it unset
# is a supported deployment: mail still sends, and `POST /api/resend/webhook`
# answers 500 to anything that reaches it rather than the app failing to boot.
#
#   RESEND_WEBHOOK_SECRET — the signing secret Resend issues when you register
#     a webhook endpoint, in its `whsec_…` form. It is what tells a genuine
#     `email.delivered` / `email.bounced` / `email.complained` /
#     `email.delivery_delayed` delivery from anything else POSTing at a public
#     URL, so without it the endpoint trusts nothing.
#
# Registering the endpoint and setting this on the deployment is MOTIR-3518 —
# console work with a credential, not something a build can do
# (on Fly: `flyctl secrets set -a motir-core RESEND_WEBHOOK_SECRET=…`).
# RESEND_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# Google OAuth (Subtask 1.1.4) — required. The Google "Continue with Google"
# button needs both. Obtain them at:
#   Google Cloud Console → APIs & Services → Credentials
#     → Create Credentials → OAuth 2.0 Client ID → Web application
# Authorized redirect URI to enter at Google:
#   {MOTIR_BASE_URL}/api/auth/callback/google
#   (local dev: http://localhost:3000/api/auth/callback/google)
# Each Motir-planned project supplies its OWN values — no shared defaults
# ship with the starter or with motir-core. Missing values throw at
# module-load time so misconfiguration surfaces loudly, not at click time.
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=

# GitHub integration (Story 7.10 · MOTIR-1498) — the OAuth user-identity grant.
# These are the OAuth half of the Motir GitHub App: the client id/secret used to
# exchange the callback code for a user access token and read the member's
# GitHub identity (`/api/github/oauth/start` → `/callback`). The App-installation
# keys (App id, private key, webhook secret) land with the installation grant
# (MOTIR-891). Register the App at GitHub → Settings → Developer settings →
# GitHub Apps; the callback URL to enter is
#   {MOTIR_BASE_URL}/api/github/oauth/callback
#   (local dev: http://localhost:3000/api/github/oauth/callback)
# Optional: unset means the GitHub connect flow is simply unreachable (the
# routes redirect with ?github=not_configured) — a self-hosted instance that
# doesn't use GitHub needs none of these and won't crash without them.
GITHUB_APP_CLIENT_ID=
GITHUB_APP_CLIENT_SECRET=
# Symmetric key that encrypts the stored GitHub user access token at rest
# (AES-256-GCM, lib/github/tokenCrypto.ts). REQUIRED whenever the GitHub OAuth
# keys above are set — the callback can't persist an identity without it.
# Provide 64 hex chars OR a 32-byte base64 value. Generate one with:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
# Rotating it makes every previously-stored GitHub token undecryptable (members
# re-connect); treat it like BETTER_AUTH_SECRET.
GITHUB_TOKEN_ENCRYPTION_KEY=

# Symmetric key that encrypts stored IMPORT-SOURCE OAuth tokens at rest (Jira /
# Linear / Plane connect flows — Story 7.16, lib/crypto/tokenCrypto.ts). OPTIONAL:
# when unset it falls back to GITHUB_TOKEN_ENCRYPTION_KEY, so a deployment that
# already configured GitHub needs no new key. Set a DEDICATED key (same 64-hex /
# 32-byte-base64 format) to isolate import tokens from the GitHub key. Rotating it
# makes previously-stored import tokens undecryptable (members re-connect).
IMPORT_TOKEN_ENCRYPTION_KEY=

# Symmetric key that encrypts a Motir repository's SECRET environment values at
# rest (Story MOTIR-9795, lib/environmentConfig/crypto.ts). 64 hex chars or a
# 32-byte base64 value, as above. It has NO fallback: unlike the import, GitLab
# and monitor keys it never falls back to GITHUB_TOKEN_ENCRYPTION_KEY, so app
# secrets rotate and leak independently. Unset, no secret can be stored. Rotating
# it makes every stored secret value undecryptable (administrators re-enter them).
ENVIRONMENT_CONFIG_ENCRYPTION_KEY=

# Linear import "Connect" OAuth app (Story 7.16 · MOTIR-1655, registered in
# MOTIR-1658). Powers the keyless Linear connect flow for the issue importer: the
# member is redirected to Linear, and the callback exchanges the code for a token
# stored ENCRYPTED via the import-source identity substrate. Register an OAuth
# application at https://linear.app/settings/api/applications; the callback URL
# to enter is
#   {MOTIR_BASE_URL}/api/import/linear/oauth/callback
#   (local dev: http://localhost:3000/api/import/linear/oauth/callback)
# Optional: unset means the Linear connect flow is simply unreachable (the routes
# redirect with ?import=linear_not_configured) — a self-hosted instance that
# doesn't import from Linear needs neither and won't crash without them.
LINEAR_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=
LINEAR_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=

# Jira import — OAuth 2.0 (3LO) connect flow (Story 7.16 · MOTIR-1654). The
# client id/secret of the Atlassian OAuth 2.0 app registered in MOTIR-943, used
# to exchange the callback code for an access + refresh token so the Jira
# connector authenticates with a STORED, encrypted Bearer token instead of a
# credential pasted into the import wizard (`/api/import/jira/oauth/start` →
# `/callback`). Register at Atlassian → Developer console → your app → Auth; the
# callback URL to enter is
#   {MOTIR_BASE_URL}/api/import/jira/oauth/callback
#   (local dev: http://localhost:3000/api/import/jira/oauth/callback)
# with the `read:jira-work` + `offline_access` scopes. Optional: unset means the
# Jira connect flow is simply unreachable (the routes redirect with
# ?jira=not_configured) — a self-hosted instance that doesn't import from Jira
# needs neither var and won't crash without them.
JIRA_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=
JIRA_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=

# Plane import — "Connect" OAuth flow (Story 7.16 · MOTIR-1656). The client
# id/secret of the Plane OAuth application registered in MOTIR-1659, used to
# exchange the callback code for an access (+ refresh) token so the Plane
# connector authenticates with a STORED, encrypted Bearer token instead of a
# PAT pasted into the import wizard (`/api/import/plane/oauth/start` →
# `/callback`). Register at Plane → workspace Settings → Integrations; the
# callback URL to enter is
#   {MOTIR_BASE_URL}/api/import/plane/oauth/callback
#   (local dev: http://localhost:3000/api/import/plane/oauth/callback)
# These power PLANE CLOUD (app.plane.so → api.plane.so, the connect form's
# default). Optional: unset means the Plane connect flow is simply unreachable
# (the routes redirect with ?import=plane_not_configured) — a self-hosted
# instance that doesn't import from Plane needs neither var and won't crash.
PLANE_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=
PLANE_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=

# Plane SELF-HOSTED per-instance OAuth apps (Story 7.16 · MOTIR-1656). Plane's
# `/auth/o/*` OAuth endpoints exist on Plane Cloud today; the open-source
# Community Edition does not yet expose them (makeplane/plane#8782). The connect
# flow still ACCEPTS a self-hosted instance base URL up front, so an instance
# that DOES register its own OAuth app can be pointed at — its client id/secret
# are resolved from this JSON map, keyed by the instance API origin (or host):
#   PLANE_OAUTH_INSTANCES={"https://plane.acme.com":{"clientId":"…","clientSecret":"…"}}
# Optional: unset (or no matching entry) means a self-hosted connect attempt
# redirects with ?import=plane_not_configured. Cloud never reads this map.
PLANE_OAUTH_INSTANCES=

# GitHub App installation grant (MOTIR-891) — "Grant 2". Server-to-server App
# credentials used to mint short-lived INSTALLATION access tokens (scoped to the
# installation's repos, NEVER stored — lib/github/appAuth.ts) and to verify
# inbound webhooks. Distinct from the OAuth identity half above. Optional: unset
# means the installation flow is simply unreachable (a self-hosted instance that
# doesn't use GitHub needs none of these and won't crash without them).
GITHUB_APP_ID=
# The App private key (PEM, from GitHub → the App → "Generate a private key").
# Newlines may be escaped as \n (restored at read time) or kept literal in a
# quoted multi-line value.
GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY=
# Shared secret configured on the GitHub App's webhook — used to verify inbound
# webhook signatures (consumed by the webhook route, MOTIR-892).
GITHUB_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# Motir's own repo-provisioning org + its "Motir Studio" App (Story MOTIR-1775 ·
# MOTIR-1779). Every repository Motir CREATES for a project lives in this org,
# and is created and written by a SECOND GitHub App installed only there — never
# by a user's token, and never by the user-facing App above (which stays
# least-privilege at Contents: read and must never gain repo-creation rights).
# All optional: unset means the creation flow is unreachable and the CI meter is
# inert. Read at CALL time, so an unwired self-host cannot crash on boot.
#
# The org's login. Doubles as the CI-metering gate: a completed workflow run is
# metered if and only if its repository's owner login matches this
# (lib/ciMetering/config.ts · docs/decisions/ci-minutes-allowance.md §5.1).
# Named "FALLBACK" from an earlier design in which a Motir-owned repo was the
# rare fallback; it is now the DEFAULT and only home for created repos. The name
# is kept because shipped code and a decision doc already read it.
GITHUB_FALLBACK_ORG=
# The "Motir Studio" App's credentials — DISTINCT from GITHUB_APP_* above, which
# is the user-facing App. This App holds Administration/Contents/Workflows write
# on Motir's org only, and is private (installable solely on the account that
# owns it), so it can never reach a repository a user owns. Same PEM newline
# handling as GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY.
GITHUB_STUDIO_APP_ID=
GITHUB_STUDIO_APP_PRIVATE_KEY=
# The token the MONTHLY CI reconciliation reads GitHub's org billing usage
# endpoint with (lib/ciMetering/config.ts · §5.8). Deliberately NOT an
# installation token: GET /organizations/{org}/settings/billing/usage is an
# ORG-level billing read that an installation token cannot perform. Unset → the
# reconciliation job no-ops with a logged reason; the webhook meter is the
# operational path and does not depend on it.
GITHUB_BILLING_TOKEN=

# GitLab integration (Story 7.23 · MOTIR-1474). GitLab is a second git provider
# behind the SAME GitProvider seam as GitHub — additive, no consumer change.
# GitLab OAuth application credentials (from the app registered in MOTIR-1473).
# Unlike GitHub's App model, GitLab's grant issues an access + refresh token per
# connection that Motir persists (encrypted) and refreshes. Optional: unset means
# the GitLab connect flow is simply unreachable (the routes redirect with
# ?gitlab=not_configured) — a self-hosted instance that doesn't use GitLab needs
# none of these and won't crash without them.
GITLAB_APP_CLIENT_ID=
GITLAB_APP_CLIENT_SECRET=
# The GitLab instance base URL (no trailing slash). OPTIONAL: defaults to
# https://gitlab.com; set it to point at a self-managed GitLab.
GITLAB_BASE_URL=
# Symmetric key that encrypts the stored GitLab access + refresh tokens at rest
# (AES-256-GCM, lib/gitlab/tokenCrypto.ts). OPTIONAL: when unset it falls back to
# GITHUB_TOKEN_ENCRYPTION_KEY, so a deployment that already configured GitHub needs
# no new key. Set a DEDICATED key (same 64-hex / 32-byte-base64 format) to isolate
# GitLab tokens. Rotating it makes previously-stored GitLab tokens undecryptable
# (members re-connect).
GITLAB_TOKEN_ENCRYPTION_KEY=
# Shared secret on the GitLab project webhook. Motir REGISTERS that hook itself when
# a project is connected, with this value as its secret token
# (lib/gitlab/projectWebhook.ts, MOTIR-5349), and the webhook route verifies every
# delivery against it (MOTIR-1475). Connecting a GitLab project is refused while it
# is unset, because a hook without it could never be verified.
GITLAB_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# Error MONITOR — Motir's PUBLIC Sentry integration, which a customer workspace
# installs on its OWN Sentry organisation (Story MOTIR-4926 · MOTIR-5260). The
# application is registered, and these values set on the deployment, by the
# provisioning card MOTIR-5257.
#
# ⚠️ NONE of these is one of MOTIR-1161's — that card wired Motir's own error
# reporting (SENTRY_DSN, SENTRY_ENVIRONMENT, SENTRY_AUTH_TOKEN, SENTRY_ORG,
# SENTRY_PROJECT) and those stay exactly as they are. The `SENTRY_APP_*` prefix is
# this feature's, and nothing here reads or rotates a value from that card.
#
# The client id + secret of the public integration. Optional: unset means the
# monitor connect flow is simply UNREACHABLE (the start route redirects with
# ?monitor=not_configured) — a self-hosted instance that never connects a monitor
# needs none of these and will not crash without them, because the credential
# resolver reads them at CALL time.
SENTRY_APP_CLIENT_ID=
SENTRY_APP_CLIENT_SECRET=
# The integration's SLUG, which is what its external-install URL is built from
# (https://sentry.io/sentry-apps/<slug>/external-install/). Optional, same
# unreachable-not-broken posture as the pair above.
SENTRY_APP_SLUG=
# Symmetric key that encrypts the stored monitor access + refresh tokens at rest
# (AES-256-GCM, lib/monitors/tokenCrypto.ts). OPTIONAL: when unset it falls back to
# GITHUB_TOKEN_ENCRYPTION_KEY, so a deployment that already configured GitHub needs
# no new key. Set a DEDICATED key (same 64-hex / 32-byte-base64 format) to isolate
# monitor tokens. Rotating it makes previously-stored monitor tokens undecryptable
# (workspaces re-connect).
SENTRY_TOKEN_ENCRYPTION_KEY=
# The provider's API root and web root. OPTIONAL, and they exist for two real
# cases rather than for symmetry: a Sentry account in a non-US DATA REGION is
# served from a different API host, and the test suite points the API root at a
# stub so it never reaches sentry.io.
SENTRY_API_BASE_URL=
SENTRY_WEB_BASE_URL=
# Make the stored `sentry` provider resolve to the in-memory FAKE
# (lib/monitors/providers/fake.ts). Set to 1 ONLY in a test environment: the E2E
# runner drives a separately-spawned server, which an in-process mock cannot
# reach, so the fake is selected by this switch instead.
MOTIR_MONITOR_FAKE_PROVIDER=

# Background jobs — the POSTGRES JOB ENGINE (Story 1.6; re-based by MOTIR-3413).
# Jobs are defined via lib/jobs/defineJob and emitted via lib/jobs/sendEvent; an
# emit writes a `job_event` + `job_queue` row, and the `worker` process group
# (`pnpm build:worker` → scripts/worker.ts) claims and runs them.
#
# ⚠️ THERE IS NOTHING TO CONFIGURE HERE, AND THAT IS THE POINT. This block used to
# carry INNGEST_DEV / INNGEST_SIGNING_KEY / INNGEST_EVENT_KEY plus a local
# dev-server command; MOTIR-3418 retired the vendor and the three variables with
# it. The engine needs the database it already has — see `docs/jobs.md` for what
# an operator does have to know (the `MOTIR_POSTGRES_JOB_IDS` cutover switch went
# with the second lane too).

# Backend AI service — used in Epic 4 to call motir-ai server-to-server.
# ⚠️ THIS IS THE ADDRESS *THIS PROCESS* REACHES motir-ai AT, and in the cloud it
# is motir-ai's PUBLIC origin (`https://motir-ai.fly.dev`) since 2026-10-02.
# motir-ai's pool suspends to zero and only the Fly proxy resumes a machine; a
# 6PN name bypasses the proxy, so it cannot wake a sleeping pool and must NOT be
# set here (docs/decisions/application-hosting.md Amendment 9, MOTIR-7407). It
# is still NOT the address an index container is given — see
# MOTIR_AI_CONTAINER_URL below.
# MOTIR_AI_URL=http://localhost:8001
# MOTIR_AI_INTERNAL_KEY=

# MOTIR_AI_CONTAINER_URL: the address a CODE-GRAPH INDEX CONTAINER is given for
# motir-ai — it becomes the container's own `MOTIR_AI_BASE_URL` (MOTIR-4518).
#
# ⚠️ IT IS A SECOND VARIABLE BECAUSE THE TWO CALLERS ARE IN DIFFERENT PLACES, and
# collapsing them into one is a real, measured outage rather than a tidiness
# question. motir-core runs in the `moooon` Fly organization; index containers run
# in the FLEET's organization (`FLY_FLEET_APP`). A `.internal` name is 6PN, so it
# resolves only within one organization — NXDOMAIN for the machine motir-core is
# booting. Between 2026-08-21 and 2026-09-04 (while MOTIR_AI_URL was that 6PN
# name) every index run downloaded its repository, spent up to two hours
# building the graph, and threw it away at `getaddrinfo ENOTFOUND` on motir-ai's
# 6PN name, one call before the upload grant, while the job ledger recorded
# success. Both variables now hold the public origin in the cloud, for different
# reasons: this one because of the org boundary, MOTIR_AI_URL because the pool
# sleeps. They stay two variables so the two callers can diverge again.
#
# Set it to an address that resolves from OUTSIDE motir-core's own organization —
# motir-ai's public ingress. The container's only authority is its run-scoped
# credential, and motir-ai's container-facing routes are gated on it, so public
# reachability is not public access.
#
# There is NO fallback to MOTIR_AI_URL: unset, `bootIndexContainer` throws before
# a machine is billed, and `system.daily-health-check` fails loudly. Leave it
# unset on a deployment that runs no index containers — the health check reports
# `not_applicable` there.
# MOTIR_AI_CONTAINER_URL=https://motir-ai.fly.dev

# Shared service bearer for motir-ai → motir-core calls (open-core boundary,
# Story 8.1). Authenticates the inbound billing-propagation route
# POST /api/internal/billing/scaled-tracker-state (8.1.4c); the SAME value is set
# on motir-ai's coreClient (8.1.4d). The route fails CLOSED when unset (rejects
# every request), so leave it unset locally unless exercising the cross-service
# path. Stripe secrets never enter motir-core — only plain subscription state.
# MOTIR_AI_TO_CORE_SERVICE_TOKEN=

# Cross-origin allowlist for the public POST /api/idea-draft receiver (Subtask
# 7.22.2 / MOTIR-1458). The standalone motir-marketing site (motir.co, a DIFFERENT
# origin) forwards a logged-out visitor's hero idea to this endpoint. List the
# exact allowed origin(s), comma-separated (no trailing slash), e.g.
# `https://motir.co,https://www.motir.co`. Leave UNSET to fail CLOSED — the
# endpoint then rejects every cross-origin caller (same-origin calls carry no
# Origin header and are unaffected). Locally set your marketing dev origin (e.g.
# http://localhost:3001) to exercise the handoff.
# MOTIR_MARKETING_ORIGIN=

# Where the signed-in rail's `Docs` row points (MOTIR-4167) — the ABSOLUTE url of
# the published documentation, e.g. `https://motir.co/docs` (the hosted
# arrangement; a self-hoster may point it at that same public documentation or at
# a mirror of their own). The documentation is no longer a page this application
# serves (MOTIR-3932), so a RELATIVE value is refused and logged rather than
# rendered. Leave UNSET and the row does not render at all — absent, not dead —
# the same treatment the Legal row gets from its manifest
# (docs/decisions/public-surface-hosts.md AMENDMENT 2 §D).
# MOTIR_DOCS_URL=

# ── The object store — attachments, avatars, acceptance artifacts ───────────
# S3-compatible (Tigris), TWO buckets: a PUBLIC one (avatars and other public
# assets — directly fetchable, public-read) and a PRIVATE one (comment and
# description embeds, panel files, the acceptance video and trace — presigned
# GET only, through /api/attachments/[id]/content). The split is structural, per
# `docs/decisions/attachment-access-control.md` Amendment 2; MOTIR-2386 creates
# both buckets and sets these seven as Fly secrets under exactly these names.
#
# Read LAZILY by `lib/blob/s3.ts`, so `next build` and any suite that does not
# touch the store needs none of them. A REAL credential is only needed to
# actually upload; the E2E lane intercepts the endpoint (lib/test-blob-mock.ts).
# On AWS, leave the endpoint and BOTH keys unset: the client then signs with the
# task role through the AWS credential chain (set both keys, or neither).
MOTIR_S3_ENDPOINT=https://fly.storage.tigris.dev
MOTIR_S3_REGION=auto
MOTIR_S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=PLACEHOLDER_access_key
MOTIR_S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=PLACEHOLDER_secret_key
MOTIR_S3_PRIVATE_BUCKET=motir-private
MOTIR_S3_PUBLIC_BUCKET=motir-public
# The public bucket's directly-fetchable origin — the prefix every public asset
# URL is built from, and the prefix an own-avatar URL is recognised by.
MOTIR_S3_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=https://fly.storage.tigris.dev/motir-public

# ── The CI runner fleet (Story MOTIR-1916) ──────────────────────────────────
# All FOUR are read by `flyFleetConfig()` in
# `lib/orchestrator/adapters/fly/flyMachines.ts`. The first three are REQUIRED:
# `isFlyFleetConfigured()` returns false without any one of them and the fleet
# boots nothing. Leave them UNSET locally — the `fake` orchestrator adapter is
# what the test suite and a local run use, and it needs none of this.
#
# MOTIR-1979 is the card that sets these in the deployment.
#
# MOTIR_RUNNER_IMAGE — the runner image every ephemeral runner boots from,
# `infra/ci-runner/`. It takes a DIGEST reference and NEVER a tag:
#
#   MOTIR_RUNNER_IMAGE=ghcr.io/motir-co/motir-ci-runner@sha256:<64 hex>
#
# §7.2 of `docs/decisions/ci-runner-fleet.md` requires the digest — these
# containers execute agent-authored customer code, and a mutable tag under that
# workload could change what runs between the boot decision and the pull. The
# digest to paste is printed by `.github/workflows/release-runner-image.yml`
# into its job summary (it also pulls the image back BY that digest and
# re-asserts it before reporting).
# MOTIR_RUNNER_IMAGE=

# FLY_FLEET_API_TOKEN — a token scoped to the fleet's OWN Fly organization,
# which §7.5 requires to be separate from motir-ai / motir-gateway's: Fly's
# private networking is org-scoped and on by default, so a fleet inside the
# production org would put every customer's CI container on `.internal` with
# Motir's own services.
# FLY_FLEET_API_TOKEN=

# FLY_FLEET_APP — the Fly app the runner Machines are created under.
# FLY_FLEET_APP=

# FLY_FLEET_REGION — optional; defaults to `iad`, co-located with Neon and
# motir-core (§11 fixes single-region).
# FLY_FLEET_REGION=iad
#
# MOTIR_TERMINAL_MASTER_KEY — the agent terminal's master key (Story MOTIR-6861;
# `docs/decisions/agent-terminal.md` Q3). Each agent machine gets
# `MOTIR_TERMINAL_KEY = HMAC-SHA256(master, instanceId)` as base64url
# (`lib/agentInstances/terminalKey.ts`), and the relay signs its one-shot token
# with the same derived key. Set the SAME value on motir-core and on the
# `motir-relay` app; at least 32 characters (e.g. `openssl rand -base64 48`).
# UNSET turns the terminal OFF: agents boot as before, with no terminal process,
# service or key, and nothing is probed. Rotating it invalidates every derived
# key; the next wake of each agent re-applies its machine config.
# MOTIR_TERMINAL_MASTER_KEY=
#
# MOTIR_RELAY_URL — where the owner's browser dials the agent terminal relay
# (Story MOTIR-6861 · MOTIR-6940; `agent-terminal.md` Q1, Q3): the ticket route
# hands it out with each ticket. Unset means `wss://relay.motir.co/v1/terminal`.
# A local relay (`pnpm build:relay && node .relay/relay.mjs`) listens on
# `ws://localhost:8080/v1/terminal`.
# MOTIR_RELAY_URL=
#
# The RELAY app (`motir-relay`, `fly.relay.toml`) itself reads DATABASE_URL,
# MOTIR_TERMINAL_MASTER_KEY, SENTRY_DSN, PORT (default 8080) and MOTIR_BASE_URL —
# the one `Origin` a browser may open a terminal from (any other is closed 4403).
#
# MOTIR_HOSTED_AGENT_IMAGE — the image every hosted agent run boots (Story
# MOTIR-683 · MOTIR-690; `docs/decisions/hosted-agent-run.md` §6), built by
# `.github/workflows/hosted-agent-image.yml`. A DIGEST, never a tag — the start
# path refuses a tag, so a publish cannot change what a running deployment boots:
#   MOTIR_HOSTED_AGENT_IMAGE=ghcr.io/motir-co/motir-hosted-agent@sha256:<64 hex>
# MOTIR_HOSTED_AGENT_IMAGE=
#
# MOTIR_FLEET_AGENT_RUNNER_PROVIDER — `aws` boots hosted runs on the agent-runner AWS pool (MOTIR-9421); unset or `fly` keeps them on Fly. Read on every admission.
# MOTIR_FLEET_AGENT_RUNNER_PROVIDER=
#
# MOTIR_FLEET_CI_RUNNER_PROVIDER — `aws` boots CI jobs on the CI runner AWS pool (MOTIR-10064); unset, empty or `fly` keeps them on Fly, and any other value keeps them on Fly with a warning. Read on every admission.
# MOTIR_FLEET_CI_RUNNER_PROVIDER=

# ── The CODE-GRAPH INDEX workload on the same fleet (Story MOTIR-1981) ───────
# Read by `indexFleetConfig()` in `lib/orchestrator/index.ts`, alongside the
# three FLY_FLEET_* variables above. The index workload shares the fleet's org,
# app and token — it needs ONE thing of its own, its image. Leave it UNSET
# locally: `isIndexFleetConfigured()` answers true for the `fake` adapter, which
# is what the test suite selects, and no image is ever pulled there.
#
# MOTIR_INDEXER_IMAGE — the container that fetches a pre-signed tarball URL,
# builds the code graph, and hands motir-ai the pointer (`motir-ai`
# `infra/indexer/`). Digest-pinned, never a tag.
#
# ⚠️ USE THE `registry.fly.io` DIGEST, NOT THE GHCR ONE:
#
#   MOTIR_INDEXER_IMAGE=registry.fly.io/<fleet-app>@sha256:<64 hex>
#
# `docs/decisions/fleet-image-pull.md` §0 measured why: the Fly Machines API
# create payload has NO field for registry authentication, so Fly cannot pull a
# private third-party image and rejects one at create time with
# `failed to get manifest …: unauthorized`. The indexer image is built from the
# CLOSED motir-ai source, so §1 puts it in the MIRROR column — the release lane
# (`motir-ai/.github/workflows/release-indexer-image.yml`) pushes to GHCR and
# then `skopeo copy`s it into `registry.fly.io`, which Fly authenticates itself.
# The copy is digest-preserving and the lane prints the reference to paste here
# into its job summary. A GHCR digest here is not a smaller mistake: it produces
# a reference no fleet machine can pull.
#
# On AWS (MOTIR-10123) core's services read it from the SSM parameter
# `/motir/core/indexer-image` that motir-infra creates, and it names Motir's own
# ECR copy instead — `<account>.dkr.ecr.<region>.amazonaws.com/motir/indexer@sha256:…`
# — which an index VM logs in to with its `motir-fleet-index` instance profile.
# MOTIR_INDEXER_IMAGE=

# ─── Public API (`/api/v1`) rate limiting ────────────────────────────────────
#
# The per-TOKEN budget every `/api/v1` route is limited by, enforced inside the
# shared route wrapper (`lib/api/v1/rateLimit.ts`) and reported on every
# response as `X-RateLimit-Limit` / `-Remaining` / `-Reset`.
#
# Both are OPTIONAL: unset — or set to a non-numeric or non-positive value —
# falls back to the documented default rather than disabling the limiter, so a
# typo in a deploy config cannot silently remove the ceiling.
#
# Defaults: 60 requests per 60_000 ms per token, matching the mirror product's
# published budget. A self-hoster's ceiling is not Motir Cloud's, which is why
# these exist; raising the budget is additive under the API's stability promise
# (`docs/decisions/public-api-conventions.md` §6 + §8).
#
# The counter is SHARED across instances, so the number below is the ceiling for
# the whole deployment rather than for each machine. Where the tally is kept is
# MOTIR_RATE_LIMIT_STORE's business, below.
# MOTIR_API_V1_RATE_LIMIT=60
# MOTIR_API_V1_RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_MS=60000
#
# The per-WORKSPACE agent-volume ceiling (MOTIR-8601): one bucket every MCP
# request and every authenticated /api/v1 request in a workspace spends beside
# its own per-credential limb, so minting tokens cannot buy past it. The MCP
# per-credential budget is MOTIR_MCP_RATE_LIMIT (default 600/min, per
# credential). Same fall-back-to-default rule as above.
# MOTIR_AGENT_WORKSPACE_RATE_LIMIT=12000
# MOTIR_AGENT_WORKSPACE_RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_MS=60000

# ─── Where every rate-limit counter is kept ──────────────────────────────────
#
# ONE store backs every limiter in the app — `/api/v1`'s per-token budget above
# and the app-level auth / public-write / AI budgets alike. They share the
# counter, never the ceiling: each keeps its own budget variables.
#
# OPTIONAL. Unset resolves to `postgres` wherever DATABASE_URL is set, and to
# `memory` where none is, so a real deployment gets the shared counter by
# default rather than by opting in. There is no vendor to configure: the store
# is the application's own database.
#
#   postgres  the shared `rate_limit_counter` table (the default). One window
#             across every process — the limit means what it says.
#   memory    an in-process counter. For tests, and for a single-instance
#             self-host that would rather not write: with one process, per-
#             process and shared are the same thing. On more than one, the
#             effective ceiling becomes `limit × instances`.
#
# ⚠️ `redis` is NOT an accepted value and an unrecognised value THROWS rather
# than falling back — a silent fallback to per-process counters would keep
# claiming a limit it was no longer enforcing. The triggers that would earn a
# Redis arm are measured ones, listed in
# `docs/decisions/production-service-stack.md` §6.
# MOTIR_RATE_LIMIT_STORE=postgres

# ─── Product analytics (Plausible) — OPTIONAL, and unset means OFF ───────────
#
# PLAUSIBLE_SCRIPT_SRC: the per-site analytics script URL. Read in exactly one
# place — lib/analytics.ts — and rendered server-side into the root layout's
# <head> (docs/decisions/production-service-stack.md §5).
#
# UNSET (the default, and what every local checkout and test run uses) means no
# tag is rendered and no analytics request is ever made. That is the self-host
# guarantee: an install that sets nothing phones nowhere. A self-hoster who
# wants analytics points this at their own Plausible instance's script URL —
# the vendor is never hardcoded.
#
# ⚠️ It is a RUNTIME variable and must stay one: never rename it to
# NEXT_PUBLIC_*, and never pass it as a Docker build argument. A NEXT_PUBLIC_*
# value is inlined into the client bundle by `next build`, which would create
# the build-argument dependency §5 chose Plausible to avoid.
# (`tests/analytics.test.tsx` asserts both.)
#
# Production is set as a Fly runtime secret:
#   flyctl secrets set -a motir-core PLAUSIBLE_SCRIPT_SRC=https://plausible.io/js/pa-<hash>.js
# PLAUSIBLE_SCRIPT_SRC=

# ─── Error monitoring (Sentry) — Subtask 8.5.6 / MOTIR-1162 ──────────────────
#
# ALL OPTIONAL, and the unset state is a supported configuration rather than a
# degraded one: with no DSN anywhere, `Sentry.init` is never called, no
# integration is installed and the app phones nowhere. That is the self-host
# path, and `tests/monitoring/sentry-init-gate.test.ts` asserts it.
#
# ⚠️ THESE VALUES DO NOT ALL LIVE IN THE SAME PLACE IN PRODUCTION, and putting
# one in the wrong home fails SILENTLY. The split is a property of how Next
# builds, not a convention:
#
#   RUNTIME (a Fly secret; read from process.env when the server boots)
#     SENTRY_DSN                     the server + Edge runtimes' own copy
#     SENTRY_ENVIRONMENT             which DEPLOYMENT this is — deliberately
#                                    runtime, so two deployments of one image
#                                    can differ without a rebuild
#     SENTRY_TRACES_SAMPLE_RATE      optional; a number 0–1, unset = 0 (no
#                                    performance tracing). Read by the WEB SERVER
#                                    and the Edge runtime only — the job worker,
#                                    the terminal relay and the browser stay at 0
#                                    whatever it says. It has a cost: see the
#                                    span-volume model on `serverTracesSampleRate`
#                                    in lib/monitoring/config.ts before setting it
#                                    (`flyctl secrets set -a <app>
#                                    SENTRY_TRACES_SAMPLE_RATE=…`).
#
#   BUILD TIME (a Docker build argument; see the Dockerfile's ARG block)
#     NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_DSN         a NEXT_PUBLIC_* value is INLINED into the
#                                    browser bundle by `next build`. A runtime
#                                    secret can never reach it — the browser
#                                    half would simply never report anything,
#                                    with nothing failing loudly.
#     NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_ENVIRONMENT optional; defaults to `production`. The
#                                    browser has no runtime env to read, so this
#                                    is the one axis that must be compiled in.
#     MOTIR_RELEASE                  the 40-char commit SHA, used as the Sentry
#                                    release. Source maps are uploaded DURING
#                                    the build and must carry the same string
#                                    the running code reports.
#     SENTRY_ORG / SENTRY_PROJECT    where the source maps go.
#
#   BUILD SECRET (never a build argument — a build argument is recorded in the
#   build's metadata, and this is a credential)
#     SENTRY_AUTH_TOKEN              mounted for the single `next build`
#                                    command; without it, source-map upload is
#                                    disabled and the build is otherwise
#                                    identical.
#
#   RUNTIME, NOT A SECRET (set in fly.toml's [env])
#     MONITORING_METRICS_PORT        optional; unset = no metrics port at all.
#                                    When set, the web server serves every
#                                    transaction's wait/hold histograms as
#                                    Prometheus text on GET /metrics at that
#                                    port (MOTIR-8553). It needs no DSN. Fly
#                                    sets it to 9091 through fly.toml and scrapes
#                                    it there; queries are in
#                                    docs/monitoring/request-db-timing.md.
#
#     MONITORING_CLOUDWATCH_NAMESPACE optional; unset or blank = nothing is
#                                    published. When set, the web server, the
#                                    job worker and the terminal relay each
#                                    print one CloudWatch embedded-metric line a
#                                    minute on stdout (EventLoopDelayMaxMs,
#                                    EventLoopDelayP99Ms) in this namespace
#                                    (MOTIR-9881). Opens no port and needs no
#                                    AWS credentials: the ECS awslogs driver
#                                    carries stdout to CloudWatch. Fly does not
#                                    set it.
#     MONITORING_CLOUDWATCH_SERVICE  the `Service` dimension on those lines
#                                    (web, worker or relay); blank publishes as
#                                    `unknown` with one warning at start.
#                                    With the namespace set, each process also
#                                    prints LoopBlockMaxMs / LoopBlockCount for
#                                    an interval in which a loop block over 1 s
#                                    ended (MOTIR-9882).
#     MONITORING_CLOUDWATCH_STEAL    optional; `true` or `1` also publishes
#                                    CpuStealMs, the growth of /proc/stat's steal
#                                    counter per minute. Set only where the host
#                                    reports steal (MOTIR-9880 read it on
#                                    Fargate). Any other value reads nothing.
#     MOTIR_BREACH_ENABLED           REHEARSAL-ONLY. `true` lets
#                                    `node breach/breach.mjs <memory|cpu|loop>`
#                                    push memory, CPU or the event loop past an
#                                    alarm's threshold for a bounded time
#                                    (MOTIR-9884). Set it only on the one-off
#                                    task of a rehearsal service, never on the
#                                    web, worker or relay; unset, the command
#                                    exits 2 and does nothing.
#
# Locally, setting SENTRY_DSN alone is enough to see server errors arrive.
# SENTRY_DSN=
# SENTRY_ENVIRONMENT=development
# SENTRY_TRACES_SAMPLE_RATE=
# MONITORING_METRICS_PORT=
# MONITORING_CLOUDWATCH_NAMESPACE=
# MONITORING_CLOUDWATCH_SERVICE=
# MONITORING_CLOUDWATCH_STEAL=
# MOTIR_BREACH_ENABLED=
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_DSN=
